export const DATA_42: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-050", num: 50, title: "Đề 050", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Importance of Reading and Writing Skills", paragraphs: ["Reading and writing are two of the most fundamental skills that a person can develop. From the moment children begin their education, these skills form the foundation of almost everything they learn. Whether it is understanding a science textbook, solving a maths problem with written instructions, or studying history through stories and documents, the ability to read and write well makes learning in every subject easier and more effective. For this reason, schools around the world place reading and writing at the very centre of the curriculum from the earliest grades.", "Reading does much more than help people understand words on a page. When people read regularly, they naturally build a larger vocabulary, which helps them express their own thoughts more clearly. Reading also improves concentration, because following a story or an argument requires the reader to stay focused for extended periods of time. Research has shown that children who read for pleasure outside of school tend to perform better in all subjects, not just in language classes. This is because reading strengthens the brain's ability to process information, make connections between ideas, and think critically about what is being said.", "Writing is equally important, and it serves many purposes in everyday life. At school, students use writing to record information, complete assignments, and demonstrate what they have learned. In adult life, writing is essential for communicating in the workplace — from sending professional emails and preparing reports to filling out important forms and writing job applications. Good writing is not just about using correct grammar and spelling; it also requires the ability to organise ideas clearly and to express thoughts in a way that is easy for others to understand. People who write well are often seen as more professional and capable in both academic and work settings.", "One of the most common reasons why people struggle with reading and writing is a lack of regular practice. Just like learning to play a musical instrument or improving at a sport, developing strong literacy skills requires consistent effort over time. Students who read only when it is required for school assignments and who avoid writing outside of class are less likely to develop the fluency and confidence that regular practice brings. Experts recommend that people of all ages set aside a small amount of time each day for reading and writing — even something as simple as keeping a personal journal or reading a short article can make a significant difference over months and years.", "Technology has changed the way many people read and write today. Digital devices such as smartphones, tablets, and computers have made reading more accessible, with millions of books, articles, and news stories available at the touch of a button. However, some researchers worry that the type of reading done on screens — often short, fast, and interrupted by notifications — is different from the deep, focused reading that builds strong comprehension skills. Similarly, the rise of instant messaging and social media has encouraged shorter and more casual forms of writing, which may not provide the same cognitive benefits as longer, more structured writing tasks.", "Parents and teachers play a crucial role in helping young people develop strong reading and writing habits. Reading aloud to young children, visiting libraries, discussing books as a family, and encouraging children to keep diaries or write stories are all practical ways to build a love of literacy from an early age. When children see the adults around them reading and writing regularly, they are more likely to view these activities as normal and enjoyable parts of daily life rather than as difficult tasks that only happen at school.", "Ultimately, the ability to read and write well is not just a school skill — it is a life skill. Strong literacy opens doors to better education, better career opportunities, and a greater ability to participate in society. In a world where information is constantly being produced and shared, the people who can read carefully, think critically, and write clearly are better equipped to navigate challenges, make informed decisions, and contribute meaningfully to the world around them."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To argue that schools spend too little time teaching reading and writing in the modern curriculum", "To compare the reading habits of children in different countries around the world", "To explain why reading and writing are essential life skills and how they can be developed and maintained", "To describe the negative effects of digital technology on young people's literacy development"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do children who read for pleasure tend to perform better in all school subjects?", options: ["Reading for pleasure teaches children specific facts that are tested in science and history exams", "Reading strengthens the brain's ability to process information, make connections, and think critically", "Children who read for pleasure also tend to spend more time studying after school", "Reading regularly gives children access to the same information their teachers use in class"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as an example of writing used in adult working life?", options: ["Writing short stories to share with colleagues", "Taking notes during university lectures and seminars", "Keeping a personal journal to record daily experiences", "Preparing professional reports and writing job applications"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do experts recommend to help people improve their literacy skills?", options: ["Setting aside a small amount of time each day for reading and writing activities", "Enrolling in a formal writing course at least once a year", "Focusing on grammar exercises rather than creative writing tasks", "Reading only books written by recognised authors"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do some researchers have about reading on digital screens?", options: ["Children who read on screens are more likely to choose entertainment over educational content", "Screen reading causes physical eye strain that permanently reduces reading ability", "Digital devices are too expensive for most students to use regularly for reading", "Screen reading tends to be short, fast, and interrupted, which may not build the same deep comprehension as focused reading"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of reading regularly?", options: ["Developing faster mathematical calculation skills", "Improving the ability to concentrate", "Building a larger vocabulary", "Performing better across all school subjects"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["The parents and teachers who guide children's reading and writing habits", "The young people and children being raised by the adults described in the paragraph", "The books and stories that children are encouraged to read at home", "The teachers who encourage students to visit libraries and read at home"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"capable\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Well-known and respected by a large number of people", "Carefully trained in formal academic writing techniques", "Willing to take on additional responsibilities at work", "Able to do things well and effectively"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"fluency\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The ability to perform a skill smoothly and with ease", "The habit of reading and writing at a fixed time every day", "A feeling of enjoyment that comes from completing a difficult task", "The speed at which a person can read a printed page"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 5 and 6 about the author's view of how reading and writing should be practised?", options: ["The author suggests that digital reading will eventually replace traditional book reading entirely", "The author implies that the quality and type of reading and writing activities matter, not just the amount of time spent on them", "The author believes that any form of reading or writing, regardless of format, provides equal cognitive benefits", "The author argues that children should be banned from using smartphones until they have mastered basic literacy skills"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Journey of Jack Ma: From Teacher to Tech Tycoon", paragraphs: ["Few business stories in recent decades have captured the world's imagination quite like that of Jack Ma, the founder of Alibaba Group. Born in 1964 in Hangzhou, China, Ma grew up in modest circumstances and showed little of the academic brilliance that is traditionally associated with great entrepreneurs. He failed his university entrance examinations twice before eventually being accepted into a teaching programme at a local college. After graduating, he worked as an English teacher, earning a salary of roughly twelve dollars a month — a humble beginning that would later become central to his public identity as a self-made success story.", "Ma's introduction to the internet came in 1995 during a trip to the United States, where he encountered the technology for the first time. Struck by the absence of any Chinese content online, he saw an immediate opportunity. Upon returning to China, he founded a small internet company that helped Chinese businesses establish an online presence. The venture struggled financially, but it gave Ma his first practical experience of the digital economy and sharpened his instinct for identifying underserved markets. By 1999, convinced that China's growing consumer economy was ready for a new kind of trading platform, Ma gathered a group of eighteen friends and colleagues in his Hangzhou apartment and founded Alibaba with a total starting capital of approximately sixty thousand US dollars.", "Alibaba's early years were marked by fierce competition and repeated near-failure. The platform faced a powerful challenge when eBay entered the Chinese market in 2003, acquiring a leading local player and pouring hundreds of millions of dollars into its operations. Rather than compete directly on eBay's terms, Ma launched Taobao — a consumer-to-consumer marketplace that offered free listings at a time when eBay charged fees for every transaction. This decision to forgo short-term revenue in order to capture market share proved decisive: within three years, Taobao had overtaken eBay in China, and eBay eventually withdrew from the market altogether. The episode became one of the most studied examples of competitive strategy in the history of Chinese business.", "The scale of Alibaba's growth over the following decade was remarkable by any measure. By 2014, when the company conducted its initial public offering on the New York Stock Exchange, it raised approximately 25 billion US dollars — at the time, the largest IPO in history. The listing made Jack Ma the wealthiest person in China and brought global attention to the extraordinary expansion of Chinese e-commerce. Alibaba's ecosystem had by then grown to encompass not only its original business-to-business trading platform but also digital payments through Alipay, cloud computing services, logistics networks, and an entertainment division — a breadth of operations that drew comparisons to Amazon, Google, and PayPal combined.", "Beyond his business achievements, Jack Ma became a widely recognised public figure whose speeches and interviews attracted large audiences in China and internationally. He was known for delivering motivational messages that drew on his own experiences of failure and persistence, and his advice to young people — including the famous observation that it is important to remain curious, to keep learning, and never to give up after setbacks — resonated deeply in a culture where educational pressure and fear of failure are pervasive. Ma served on several international advisory boards and was regarded by many governments and organisations as a symbol of what entrepreneurial ambition could achieve in a developing economy.", "His later years, however, brought considerable turbulence. In late 2020, Chinese regulators halted the planned initial public offering of Ant Group — Alibaba's financial technology affiliate — in what was widely interpreted as a regulatory response to a speech Ma had delivered criticising China's financial regulatory system. The episode triggered a sharp fall in Alibaba's share price and marked a significant shift in Ma's public profile. He largely withdrew from public view in the months that followed, and his reduced visibility fuelled international speculation about the relationship between China's government and its most prominent private-sector entrepreneurs.", "Jack Ma's story resists simple conclusions. It is simultaneously a narrative of individual determination triumphing over repeated failure, a case study in the competitive dynamics of global digital commerce, and a reminder that even the most celebrated business careers can be reshaped — abruptly and unexpectedly — by forces beyond the entrepreneur's control. For students of business, economics, and modern Chinese history alike, his journey from a twelve-dollar-a-month schoolteacher to the architect of one of the world's largest commercial ecosystems remains one of the defining stories of the early twenty-first century."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The impact of Chinese government policy on the technology industry during the 2010s", "The life and career of Jack Ma, from his humble beginnings through his business achievements to the challenges of his later years", "The development of e-commerce regulations in China during the early twenty-first century", "A comparison of Alibaba's business model with those of Amazon and Google in global digital markets"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes Jack Ma's early life and education?", options: ["He grew up in a wealthy family that provided him with connections in the business world", "He studied computer science at a leading Chinese university before beginning his career in technology", "He excelled academically and was accepted into a prestigious university on his first attempt", "He failed his university entrance exams twice and later worked as an English teacher earning a very low salary"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the key strategic decision that allowed Taobao to overtake eBay in China?", options: ["Taobao invested more heavily in advertising than eBay during the critical years of market competition", "Taobao partnered with the Chinese government to block eBay from operating in the country", "Taobao offered free listings while eBay charged transaction fees, attracting more sellers to the platform", "Taobao focused exclusively on business-to-business trade, avoiding direct competition with eBay's consumer market"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was historically significant about Alibaba's stock market listing in 2014?", options: ["It was the first time a Chinese company had been allowed to list on the New York Stock Exchange", "It resulted in Alibaba acquiring several major American technology companies as part of its global expansion", "It raised approximately 25 billion US dollars, making it the largest initial public offering in history at the time", "It marked the first time a Chinese entrepreneur had been named the wealthiest person in the world"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did Jack Ma's public speeches resonate deeply with young people in China?", options: ["He promised financial rewards to young entrepreneurs who followed his business methods", "His messages about curiosity, learning, and persistence after failure spoke to a culture where educational pressure and fear of failure are widespread", "He criticised the Chinese education system, which made him popular among students who disliked school", "He regularly appeared on television programmes that reached millions of viewers across China"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of Alibaba's business ecosystem by 2014?", options: ["Online education platforms for students and professionals", "Cloud computing services", "A logistics and delivery network", "Digital payment services through Alipay"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"He\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["Jack Ma, who withdrew from public view following the regulatory intervention and share price fall", "An unnamed government official who delivered a public response to Ma's criticisms", "The chief executive officer of Ant Group who replaced Jack Ma after the IPO was halted", "A Chinese financial regulator who investigated Alibaba's business practices"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"struggled\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Operated quietly without attracting significant public interest", "Expanded quickly into new markets despite limited resources", "Attracted the attention of investors looking for high-growth opportunities", "Faced serious difficulties and worked hard to overcome them"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"forgo\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To delay a financial decision until market conditions become more favourable", "To invest heavily in a new market in order to gain a competitive advantage", "To deliberately give up or do without something, usually for a greater benefit", "To negotiate a compromise between two competing business objectives"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward Jack Ma's story as expressed in paragraph 7?", options: ["Pessimistic, implying that entrepreneurial success in China is ultimately impossible without government approval", "Strongly critical, suggesting that Ma's downfall proves the dangers of confronting government authority", "Balanced and analytical, acknowledging both the inspirational dimensions of Ma's career and its complexity and unpredictability", "Entirely admiring, presenting Ma as a flawless model of entrepreneurial success that others should imitate"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Global Dominance of the English Language", paragraphs: ["No language in recorded history has achieved the global reach that English commands today. Spoken natively by approximately 380 million people and used as a second or foreign language by an estimated 1.5 billion more, English functions as the primary medium of international diplomacy, scientific publication, business negotiation, digital communication, and popular entertainment across virtually every region of the world. This ascendancy did not emerge naturally from any inherent superiority of the language itself — linguists are broadly agreed that no language is objectively better suited to complex thought or communication than any other — but from a specific historical sequence of military conquest, colonial expansion, technological innovation, and economic dominance that elevated first Britain and then the United States to positions of global influence.", "The foundations of English's international spread were laid during the British Empire's period of greatest territorial expansion, roughly from the seventeenth to the early twentieth century. At its peak, the Empire encompassed roughly one quarter of the world's land surface, and British colonial administration imposed English-medium education systems across territories from the Indian subcontinent to sub-Saharan Africa to the Caribbean. The consequences of this imposition were profound and long-lasting: even after formal decolonisation, English retained official status in many former colonies, embedded not only in government and law but in the educational aspirations of millions of families who recognised that proficiency in English offered access to higher education, professional advancement, and upward social mobility that was difficult to obtain through local languages alone.", "The post-war rise of the United States consolidated and extended English's dominance in new domains. American cultural exports — Hollywood films, popular music, television programmes, and later the internet and its overwhelmingly Anglophone content infrastructure — spread English-language cultural products into homes and schools on every continent. The emergence of English as the default language of international scientific publishing, epitomised by the dominance of English-language journals in every major academic discipline, meant that researchers whose native language was not English faced a systemic disadvantage: they had to master not only their subject matter but also the conventions of academic writing in a second language in order to participate fully in global knowledge production.", "Critics of English's global dominance have raised concerns that go well beyond questions of linguistic inconvenience. Some linguists and cultural theorists argue that the hegemony of English in global communication systematically disadvantages speakers of other languages, not merely by imposing a practical burden but by privileging the conceptual frameworks, rhetorical conventions, and cultural assumptions embedded in the English-speaking world. When international negotiations are conducted in English, when scientific findings are published in English, and when the platforms through which ideas circulate are architected primarily for Anglophone users, the argument runs, the perspectives and knowledge systems of non-Anglophone communities are not simply translated — they are structurally marginalised. The loss of linguistic diversity at the global level is also linked by researchers to the erosion of the cognitive and cultural diversity that different language communities embody and transmit.", "A further dimension of the critique concerns the internal diversity of English itself. The version of English that functions as an international standard — sometimes called World English or Global English — is not a neutral or cultureless medium but one that bears the historical imprint of its dominant varieties: primarily British and American English, with their attendant class associations, regional preferences, and stylistic norms. Non-native speakers who master this standard variety may do so at the cost of linguistic identities rooted in their own regional, national, or community dialects. In many postcolonial societies, debates about the role of English in education continue to generate intense controversy, as governments and communities attempt to balance the practical advantages of English proficiency against the imperative of preserving and developing indigenous language traditions.", "Despite the mounting critiques, predictions of English's imminent decline have repeatedly proved premature. The rise of Mandarin Chinese as a major global language, though significant, has not displaced English as the preferred medium of international transactions in most sectors. The expansion of the internet, rather than democratising global communication across languages as some early theorists hoped, has in many respects reinforced English's dominance by establishing it as the default language of the most widely used digital platforms, coding environments, and knowledge repositories. Whether this dominance will persist through the twenty-first century — or whether advances in machine translation and the growing assertiveness of non-Anglophone cultures will eventually erode it — remains genuinely uncertain. [1]", "What is clear is that English's global position is neither natural nor inevitable — it is a historical and political construction that continues to evolve. [2] Understanding the forces that produced it, and the inequalities it sustains, is a precondition for any serious engagement with questions of global communication, educational equity, and the right of all human communities to participate fully in the production and circulation of knowledge. [3] Those who benefit most from English's dominance are often least aware of the privilege it confers, while those who bear its costs most heavily — the billions who must navigate the world in a language not their own — are frequently the least able to influence the institutional arrangements that perpetuate it. [4]"], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["English's global dominance is a product of historical and political forces that have created both practical advantages and significant inequalities across language communities.", "Predictions that English will decline as a global language have been confirmed by the rapid rise of Mandarin Chinese.", "The expansion of the internet has made English the only viable language for international communication in the twenty-first century.", "English has become the world's dominant language due to inherent linguistic qualities that make it easier to learn than other major languages."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did English retain official status in many countries even after they gained independence from Britain?", options: ["English was retained because former colonial populations had come to associate proficiency in it with education, professional success, and social advancement.", "British development aid was conditional on former colonies maintaining English as an official language of government.", "International organisations such as the United Nations required all member states to conduct official business in English.", "Newly independent governments chose English because it was considered politically neutral and less associated with any single ethnic group."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"ascendancy\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a gradual process of linguistic evolution driven by natural changes in grammar and vocabulary", "an academic study of the historical spread of a language across regions", "a position of dominance or superiority held over others", "the formal recognition of a language by international governing bodies"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what systemic disadvantage do non-English-speaking researchers face in global academic publishing?", options: ["They are legally prevented from submitting work to the most prestigious international journals without co-authoring with a native English speaker.", "Their research findings are automatically translated into English by journal publishers, introducing errors that damage scientific credibility.", "They must achieve proficiency in both their subject area and the conventions of academic writing in English to participate fully in global knowledge production.", "Universities in English-speaking countries refuse to hire researchers who have not published in English-language journals."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 (\"the perspectives and knowledge systems of non-Anglophone communities are not simply translated — they are structurally marginalised\") refers to _______.", options: ["the platforms through which ideas circulate globally", "international negotiations conducted in English", "the perspectives and knowledge systems of non-Anglophone communities", "scientific findings published in English-language journals"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what argument do critics make about conducting international negotiations in English?", options: ["It privileges the conceptual frameworks and cultural assumptions of the English-speaking world, structurally marginalising non-Anglophone perspectives.", "It discourages non-English-speaking nations from participating in international forums, reducing the diversity of policy solutions.", "It leads to frequent diplomatic misunderstandings because complex political concepts cannot be adequately expressed in English.", "It gives English-speaking nations an unfair economic advantage in trade negotiations by reducing the cost of interpretation."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"rooted\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["rigidly fixed and unable to adapt to changing social conditions", "deeply connected to or originating from a particular source or place", "formally documented and preserved in written historical records", "recently developed as a response to the pressures of globalisation"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how has the expansion of the internet affected the position of English as a global language?", options: ["It has created an equal playing field for all languages by automatically providing real-time translation for all online content.", "It has replaced English as the primary medium of international communication with a system of universal visual symbols.", "It has established English as the default language of the most widely used digital platforms, reinforcing rather than reducing its global dominance.", "It has weakened English's dominance by enabling previously marginalised languages to reach global audiences for the first time."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a form of American cultural influence that spread English globally?", options: ["The dominance of English in international scientific publishing.", "Television programmes exported to homes on every continent.", "Hollywood films and popular music distributed internationally.", "American sporting events broadcast to non-English-speaking audiences worldwide."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet the very ubiquity of English also generates a paradox: the more universal it becomes, the more invisible its cultural assumptions appear to those who use it most fluently.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Humanity's Next Frontier: The Colonization of Mars", paragraphs: ["The prospect of establishing a permanent human presence on Mars has moved, over the past two decades, from the realm of science fiction into the active planning horizons of both government space agencies and private aerospace companies. Driven by advances in rocket propulsion, life-support engineering, and in-situ resource utilisation — the capacity to manufacture essential materials from substances available on the Martian surface — proponents argue that a sustained human settlement on Mars is not merely technically conceivable but increasingly within reach of the current generation. Yet the journey from technical feasibility to actual colonisation involves a cascade of challenges — physiological, psychological, economic, and ethical — whose magnitude is frequently underestimated in the public discourse surrounding Mars exploration.", "The physical challenges of a crewed Mars mission begin long before any astronaut sets foot on Martian soil. The journey itself, depending on orbital alignment, takes between six and nine months in each direction, during which crew members are continuously exposed to cosmic radiation at levels far exceeding those encountered in low Earth orbit. The International Space Station's radiation shielding, which represents the current state of the art in crewed spacecraft protection, reduces but does not eliminate this exposure; prolonged radiation doses of the kind unavoidable on a Mars transit are associated with significantly elevated risks of cancer, damage to the central nervous system, and [1] impairment of cognitive function. [2] Microgravity exposure over the same duration causes substantial loss of bone density and muscle mass, cardiovascular deconditioning, and fluid shifts toward the head that affect vision and intracranial pressure. [3] Upon arrival, colonists would face a Martian environment characterised by surface atmospheric pressure less than one percent of Earth's, surface temperatures ranging from approximately minus 125 degrees Celsius at the poles to a maximum of around 20 degrees Celsius near the equator on summer days, and pervasive fine dust that poses risks to both equipment and respiratory health. [4]", "The psychological demands of long-duration Mars missions are no less formidable than the physical ones. Crew members would be isolated from Earth not only by physical distance but by a communication delay that, at maximum orbital separation, exceeds twenty minutes in each direction — making real-time conversation with family members, friends, or ground-based medical and psychological support staff impractical in any meaningful sense. Studies of analogous isolation environments — including Antarctic research stations, submarine deployments, and simulated Mars habitat experiments such as the Mars-500 project — have documented a consistent pattern of psychological effects: initial enthusiasm giving way to boredom, interpersonal friction, sleep disruption, and subclinical depression at rates that increase with mission duration. Designing crew selection criteria, mission architectures, and habitat environments that can reliably sustain psychological health across multi-year missions remains one of the most poorly understood challenges in human spaceflight research.", "The economic dimensions of Mars colonisation are equally daunting. Even the most optimistic cost projections for a crewed Mars mission place the figure in the hundreds of billions of dollars for a single initial landing, and the sustained presence required to constitute a genuine colony would multiply that figure by orders of magnitude. Advocates of commercial Mars development, most prominently Elon Musk's SpaceX, argue that radical reductions in launch cost — achieved through fully reusable rocket systems — can bring the economics of Mars settlement within the range of plausibility. Critics, however, point out that cost projections for large-scale space programmes have historically proved severely optimistic, that the ongoing maintenance costs of a Mars colony in an environment that provides no breathable air, no liquid water at the surface, and minimal radiation protection would be extraordinary, and that the opportunity cost of the required investment — measured against the scale of unmet human needs on Earth — constitutes a serious ethical objection that advocates rarely address with sufficient rigour.", "That ethical objection has multiple dimensions. The most immediate concerns the justice of allocating the resources, talent, and institutional attention required for Mars colonisation at a time when climate change, poverty, disease, and social inequality present existential or near-existential challenges to the survival and wellbeing of billions of people currently alive on Earth. A second, longer-range ethical concern involves the possibility of contaminating Mars with terrestrial microorganisms — a prospect that would permanently compromise the scientific integrity of the search for indigenous Martian life. Planetary protection protocols, developed under the auspices of the Committee on Space Research, attempt to manage this risk, but critics argue that the commercial timelines and cost pressures driving Mars development make rigorous adherence to these protocols increasingly difficult to guarantee in practice.", "Proponents of Mars colonisation respond to these concerns with a range of arguments. The most frequently invoked is the argument from existential diversification: that the long-term survival of humanity requires it to become a multi-planetary species, since a single catastrophic event — whether an asteroid impact, a pandemic, or a nuclear exchange — could extinguish all life on Earth if no off-world population exists. Others argue that the technologies developed in pursuit of Mars colonisation will generate spillover benefits for life on Earth in areas ranging from sustainable agriculture and water recycling to advanced medicine and materials science. Whether these arguments sufficiently justify the costs and risks involved, or whether they amount to a rationalisation for an undertaking driven primarily by technological ambition rather than genuine humanitarian calculus, is a question that philosophers, economists, and scientists continue to debate with increasing urgency as the technical barriers to Mars exploration continue to fall."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["While Mars colonisation is becoming technically more feasible, it presents a cascade of physiological, psychological, economic, and ethical challenges that are frequently underestimated.", "The ethical objections to Mars colonisation are so overwhelming that no serious scientist or philosopher supports the endeavour.", "Mars colonisation is technically impossible and should be abandoned in favour of developing better life on Earth.", "Private aerospace companies have already solved the main technical barriers to Mars colonisation and are ready to launch within a decade."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"magnitude\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the formal measurement of distance between two planetary bodies", "the financial costs associated with a large-scale infrastructure project", "the great size or scale of something, especially challenges or forces", "the specific technical details of a complex engineering problem"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one physiological effect of prolonged microgravity exposure during a Mars transit?", options: ["It induces a state of metabolic dormancy that reduces the crew's need for food and water during the journey.", "It causes permanent damage to the reproductive system that cannot be reversed after returning to Earth.", "It leads to substantial loss of bone density and muscle mass, as well as cardiovascular deconditioning.", "It accelerates the aging process, causing astronauts to age approximately three times faster than people on Earth."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"these protocols\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the commercial timelines and cost-reduction strategies developed by private aerospace companies", "the planetary protection protocols developed under the Committee on Space Research to prevent biological contamination of Mars", "the communication procedures established between Mars colonists and Earth-based mission control teams", "the ethical guidelines governing the allocation of resources between Earth-based and space-based scientific research"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the communication delay between Earth and Mars affect colonists?", options: ["It forces colonists to make all medical decisions independently because no medical data can be transmitted between planets.", "It makes emergency evacuation of the Martian colony impossible to coordinate in the event of a life-threatening crisis.", "It causes severe disorientation because colonists lose their sense of time without real-time synchronisation with Earth.", "It prevents real-time conversation with family members, friends, and support staff, making meaningful communication impractical."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"address\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to deal with or respond to a problem or criticism directly", "to deliver a formal speech in front of a large public audience", "to write the destination details on an envelope or package", "to forward a message to the correct recipient through official channels"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what risk do planetary protection critics highlight regarding commercial Mars development?", options: ["Private spacecraft are not required to meet the same safety standards as government missions, increasing the risk of catastrophic accidents.", "Cost pressures and commercial timelines may make it increasingly difficult to adhere rigorously to protocols that prevent biological contamination.", "Commercial companies may attempt to extract Martian minerals for profit, permanently altering the planet's geological structure.", "The rapid pace of commercial development will make it impossible to conduct the baseline scientific surveys needed before colonisation."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the argument from existential diversification used to justify Mars colonisation?", options: ["A Mars colony will provide a testing ground for democratic governance systems that can subsequently be applied on Earth.", "The technologies required to colonise Mars will create profitable new industries that will fund solutions to poverty and disease.", "Becoming a multi-planetary species is necessary for long-term human survival, since a single catastrophe could extinguish all life on Earth.", "Establishing a Mars colony will generate enough scientific discoveries to solve the most pressing challenges facing humanity on Earth."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'These compounding physiological risks mean that by the time a crew arrives on Mars, their bodies will already have been significantly compromised by the journey itself.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit that proponents claim Mars colonisation would bring?", options: ["Advances in medicine and materials science as spillover benefits of Mars-related research.", "The development of technologies with applications in sustainable agriculture and water recycling.", "The extraction and export of rare Martian minerals to alleviate resource shortages on Earth.", "The long-term survival of humanity through becoming a multi-planetary species."], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-085", num: 85, title: "Đề 085", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Living Near Volcanoes", paragraphs: ["Volcanoes are among the most powerful and dramatic natural forces on Earth. When a volcano erupts, it can release enormous amounts of lava, ash, and toxic gases that destroy everything in their path. Despite these obvious dangers, hundreds of millions of people around the world choose to live close to active volcanoes. In countries such as Indonesia, Japan, Italy, and the Philippines, entire cities and farming communities have been built in the shadow of volcanoes that could erupt at any time. Understanding why so many people live in such risky places requires looking at both the dangers and the surprising benefits that volcanoes can provide.", "One of the main reasons people settle near volcanoes is the exceptional quality of the surrounding soil. Over thousands of years, volcanic ash and lava break down into minerals that make the soil extraordinarily fertile . Farmers who live on or near the slopes of a volcano can often grow crops that produce much higher yields than farmers in other regions. Rice, coffee, grapes, and vegetables all thrive in volcanic soil, which is rich in nutrients such as potassium, phosphorus, and calcium. In places like the island of Java in Indonesia and the slopes of Mount Etna in Sicily, Italy, volcanic land has supported dense human populations for centuries precisely because it is so productive.", "Volcanoes also attract a large number of tourists, which can bring significant economic benefits to local communities. People travel from all over the world to witness the dramatic landscapes created by volcanic activity, including lava fields, hot springs, volcanic craters, and geysers. In Iceland, for example, geothermal energy from volcanic activity is used to heat homes and generate electricity, making it one of the most energy-efficient countries in the world. In Hawaii, Volcanoes National Park draws millions of visitors every year, supporting thousands of local jobs in hospitality, transport, and retail. For communities that might otherwise struggle to find economic opportunities, a nearby volcano can be an important asset .", "However, living near a volcano also carries very real dangers that cannot be ignored. A sudden eruption can destroy homes, farmland, and entire villages within hours. In 1985, the eruption of the Nevado del Ruiz volcano in Colombia triggered a massive mudflow that buried the town of Armero and killed more than 23,000 people. More recently, the 2018 eruption of Kilauea in Hawaii destroyed over 700 homes and forced thousands of residents to evacuate their neighborhoods. Volcanic ash, which can fall like snow during an eruption, damages crops, clogs engines, and can cause serious breathing problems for people and animals alike.", "Governments in volcano-prone regions have developed a range of strategies to help communities manage these risks. Volcanologists — scientists who study volcanoes — monitor changes in ground movement, gas emissions, and temperature around active volcanoes to detect early warning signs of an eruption. When danger appears to be rising, authorities can issue evacuation orders to move people out of harm's way before disaster strikes. In Japan and Indonesia, where earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are common, schools regularly conduct drills so that students and teachers know exactly what to do in an emergency. These precautions have saved many lives over the years.", "In the end, the decision to live near a volcano often comes down to a deeply personal calculation of risk and reward. For many people, the fertile land, the strong community ties, and the cultural connection to a place where their families have lived for generations outweigh the ever-present threat of eruption. As one farmer living on the slopes of Mount Merapi in Indonesia once explained, \"The volcano gives us life. Without it, this land would be nothing.\" For communities like his, leaving is simply not a realistic option, and so learning to live alongside the volcano — carefully, respectfully, and with one eye always watching the sky — becomes the only way forward."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The scientific methods that volcanologists use to predict volcanic eruptions accurately.", "How governments in volcano-prone countries have successfully eliminated the risks of living near an active volcano.", "Why millions of people continue to live near volcanoes despite the dangers, and the benefits that make this worthwhile.", "A comparison of the most destructive volcanic eruptions in history and the number of lives they claimed."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["A nearby volcano.", "Geothermal energy produced by volcanic activity.", "Volcanoes National Park in Hawaii.", "The tourism industry in local communities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which two places are mentioned as examples of areas where volcanic soil has supported large human populations for centuries?", options: ["The rice fields of Japan and the grape-growing regions of southern France.", "The island of Bali in Indonesia and the slopes of Mount Vesuvius in Italy.", "The highlands of the Philippines and the volcanic coast of Iceland.", "The island of Java in Indonesia and the slopes of Mount Etna in Sicily."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened when the Nevado del Ruiz volcano erupted in Colombia in 1985?", options: ["A giant ash cloud covered the country and caused widespread respiratory illness.", "A mudflow buried the town of Armero, killing more than 23,000 people.", "A massive lava flow destroyed the capital city and disrupted the national economy for years.", "Thousands of residents were evacuated safely before the eruption reached its peak."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was one consequence of the 2018 eruption of Kilauea in Hawaii?", options: ["A layer of volcanic ash three meters deep covered the island's farmland.", "The eruption triggered a tsunami that damaged coastal towns across the Pacific.", "Over 700 homes were destroyed and thousands of residents had to evacuate.", "The national park was permanently closed to tourists following the disaster."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do volcanologists monitor to detect early warning signs of an eruption?", options: ["Changes in ground movement, gas emissions, and temperature around active volcanoes.", "Changes in local weather patterns, rainfall levels, and river water temperature.", "The frequency and magnitude of earthquakes recorded within a fifty-kilometer radius.", "The migration behavior of animals living on and around the volcano's slopes."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an economic benefit provided by volcanoes to local communities?", options: ["Jobs created by national parks that attract millions of visitors.", "Revenue from tourists who visit volcanic landscapes.", "The use of geothermal energy for heating and electricity.", "Income from selling volcanic rock and minerals to construction companies."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"fertile\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Rich in nutrients and highly productive for growing crops.", "Extremely hot and difficult to work with farming tools.", "Dark in color due to the presence of volcanic minerals.", "Soft and easy to dig but poor in natural nutrients."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"precautions\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Actions taken after a disaster to help people recover and rebuild.", "Scientific studies conducted to understand the causes of volcanic eruptions.", "Measures taken in advance to prevent or reduce the risk of harm.", "Rules created by governments to limit construction near active volcanoes."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To describe the work of volcanologists and how their research has made volcanic eruptions predictable.", "To persuade governments to relocate all communities living within ten kilometers of an active volcano.", "To argue that people who live near volcanoes are making an irresponsible and dangerous choice.", "To explain in a balanced way why people choose to live near volcanoes and what the risks and benefits involve."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Passport Rules for Children Under Sixteen: What Parents Need to Know", paragraphs: ["International travel has become a normal part of modern family life, but for parents planning to take young children abroad, the rules surrounding children's passports can be surprisingly complex and are frequently misunderstood . In many countries, the regulations governing the issue and use of passports for children under sixteen differ substantially from those applying to adults, reflecting both child protection concerns and the legal recognition that minors cannot independently consent to international travel. Understanding these rules before booking a trip can save families considerable time, stress, and expense.", "In most countries, children under sixteen cannot be included on a parent's passport and must hold their own individual travel document. This requirement was introduced in many nations during the 1990s and 2000s as part of broader international efforts to prevent child abduction and trafficking across borders. A child's passport typically has a shorter validity period than an adult's passport: in the United Kingdom, for example, a child's passport is valid for five years, compared to ten years for an adult. In the United States, passports issued to children under sixteen are also valid for only five years. Parents should plan ahead to ensure that a child's passport does not expire in the middle of a planned trip, since many countries require that a passport remain valid for at least six months beyond the traveler's intended departure date.", "One of the most important and frequently overlooked requirements relates to parental consent. In a growing number of countries, a child traveling internationally without both parents must carry documentation proving that the absent parent or parents have given their consent to the trip. In Canada, for example, the government strongly recommends that a child traveling with only one parent carry a signed and notarized letter of consent from the non-traveling parent, along with that parent's contact information. South Africa goes further, requiring any minor traveling internationally — whether accompanied by one parent, a grandparent, a school group, or any other guardian — to carry an unabridged birth certificate in addition to a valid passport, a requirement that has caught many foreign visitors by surprise.", "The application process for a child's passport also differs in important ways from the adult process. In the United Kingdom, both parents with parental responsibility must countersign a child's passport application, even if the child lives primarily with only one parent. In the United States, children under sixteen applying for a passport for the first time must apply in person at an authorized passport acceptance facility, and both parents must be present at the time of application unless one parent provides a notarized statement of consent or proof of sole legal custody. These requirements are designed to ensure that a child's passport cannot be obtained without the knowledge and agreement of both legal guardians, reducing the risk of international parental abduction.", "For families where parents are divorced or separated, the passport application process can become particularly contentious . In cases where one parent objects to the child traveling internationally, they may have the legal right to prevent the passport from being issued or the child from leaving the country. In the United States, for instance, a parent can submit a form to the State Department requesting that their child be placed on the Children's Passport Issuance Alert Program, which notifies them if a passport application is submitted for that child. Courts in many countries can also issue orders restricting a child's international travel as part of custody proceedings, and violating such an order by taking a child abroad without permission can constitute a criminal offense.", "Despite the complexity of these rules, the underlying purpose is straightforward: to protect children from being taken across international borders without the consent of both parents or legal guardians. For the vast majority of families, the additional documentation requirements amount to little more than a minor administrative inconvenience. Gathering the necessary paperwork in advance — and verifying the entry requirements of the destination country, which may impose additional rules beyond those of the child's home country — can make the difference between a smooth departure and a stressful one. Travel advisors and government websites are generally the most reliable sources for up-to-date information, since regulations in this area change more frequently than many parents realize.", "In an era of increasing global mobility, the rules surrounding children's passports reflect a careful and ongoing attempt to balance two competing values: the freedom of families to travel internationally and the responsibility of states to protect the welfare of their youngest and most vulnerable citizens. Getting those rules right — and communicating them clearly to the parents who need to follow them — remains one of the quieter but more consequential challenges of modern immigration administration."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A comparison of passport application fees for children in different countries around the world.", "A guide to the best travel destinations for families with young children who have limited passport validity.", "An argument that current passport regulations for children are unnecessarily complicated and should be simplified.", "An informative overview of the passport rules and travel requirements that apply to children under sixteen and their parents."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, for how long is a child's passport valid in both the United Kingdom and the United States?", options: ["Five years.", "Three years.", "Seven years.", "Ten years, the same as an adult's passport."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what additional document does South Africa require minors to carry when traveling internationally?", options: ["A copy of the accompanying adult's legal guardianship papers issued by a court.", "An unabridged birth certificate in addition to a valid passport.", "A signed letter of consent from both parents, notarized by an official legal authority.", "A medical certificate confirming that the child is fit to travel internationally."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the Children's Passport Issuance Alert Program in the United States allow a parent to do?", options: ["Automatically block a passport application made for their child without their knowledge.", "Track the location of their child's passport in real time using a government app.", "Receive a notification if a passport application is submitted for their child.", "Apply for emergency travel documents for a child in fewer than twenty-four hours."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is required in the United Kingdom when applying for a child's passport?", options: ["The child must attend an in-person interview at a passport office with both parents.", "Only the parent with whom the child primarily lives needs to sign the application.", "Both parents with parental responsibility must countersign the application.", "A doctor or school official must provide a written statement confirming the child's identity."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure used to prevent children from being taken abroad without both parents' consent?", options: ["A government program that alerts a parent when a passport application is made for their child.", "A mandatory waiting period of thirty days before a child's passport is processed and issued.", "Court orders issued during custody proceedings that restrict a child's international travel.", "Requiring both parents to countersign a child's passport application."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"misunderstood\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Incorrectly interpreted or not properly understood.", "Written in legal language that is too difficult for ordinary people to follow.", "Recently changed and not yet widely known.", "Deliberately ignored by parents who find them inconvenient."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"unabridged\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Recently issued within the past twelve months.", "Officially stamped and verified by a government authority.", "Complete and containing all original information without any details removed.", "Translated into the official language of the destination country."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"contentious\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Expensive and requiring the services of a professional immigration lawyer.", "Time-consuming but ultimately resolved without legal intervention.", "Likely to cause disagreement or dispute between the parties involved.", "Straightforward and easy to complete with the right documents."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the reason why children's passport rules are so detailed and complex?", options: ["The complexity reflects how seriously countries treat the protection of children from being moved across borders without full parental consent.", "Passport agencies introduced additional requirements to reduce the administrative burden of processing large numbers of family applications.", "The rules became complex because different countries failed to agree on a single international standard for children's travel documents.", "Governments have made the rules complex in order to generate revenue from application fees and document processing."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Global Popularity of Football: More Than Just a Game", paragraphs: ["No sport on Earth commands a following comparable to association football — known simply as football in most of the world and as soccer in the United States and Canada. With an estimated 3.5 billion fans spread across virtually every country on the planet, football occupies a place in global culture that transcends sport and enters the territory of shared human experience. [1] Its rules are simple enough to be understood by a child in minutes, its equipment requirements minimal enough to be met with a ball and two improvised goalposts, and its emotional register wide enough to encompass ecstasy, heartbreak, communal pride, and national identity within the space of ninety minutes. These qualities help explain why football has become not merely the world's most popular sport but, arguably, its most powerful cultural institution.", "The modern game was codified in England in 1863, when the Football Association drew up the first standardized rules and separated the sport from the rougher, handling-based game that would become rugby. From England, the sport spread with remarkable speed along the routes of British trade and imperial influence — to South America, where it took root with particular intensity in Argentina and Brazil; to continental Europe, where cities such as Barcelona, Milan, and Lisbon developed club cultures of extraordinary passion; and eventually to every inhabited continent on Earth. The FIFA World Cup, first held in Uruguay in 1930, gave the sport its definitive global stage, and its growth since then has been exponential: the 2022 tournament in Qatar was watched by an estimated 1.5 billion people in a single day.", "Football's emotional power derives in large part from its structure as a low-scoring game in which chance plays an unusually prominent role. A single goal can transform a match; a single mistake by a goalkeeper can eliminate a nation from a tournament it had spent four years preparing for. [2] This precariousness — the sense that anything can happen until the final whistle — generates a level of sustained tension that higher-scoring sports struggle to replicate. It also means that genuinely weaker teams can defeat stronger opponents, a feature that produces the upsets and surprises that are among the sport's most beloved and democratizing characteristics. When Cameroon defeated Argentina at the 1990 World Cup, or when Iceland held Portugal in the 2016 European Championship, the reverberations were felt far beyond the stadium.", "The club football ecosystem that has developed around the sport is a phenomenon of staggering economic scale. The English Premier League, widely regarded as the most commercially successful domestic competition in the world, generated revenues of approximately £6.5 billion in the 2022–23 season, with broadcast rights sold to networks in over 190 countries. Top players command salaries that place them among the highest-paid individuals in any profession globally: Lionel Messi's move to Paris Saint-Germain in 2021 was reported to involve a total package exceeding €100 million per year. Transfer fees for elite players have reached levels that would have seemed entirely fantastical a generation ago, with Neymar's transfer from Barcelona to Paris Saint-Germain in 2017 setting a record of €222 million that has yet to be surpassed.", "Critics of modern football's economic model have raised concerns that are difficult to dismiss. The concentration of financial resources among a small number of superclubs — enabled by the influx of sovereign wealth fund investment from Gulf states and the structural advantages of existing commercial dominance — has progressively eroded the competitive balance that gives the sport much of its meaning. [3] In domestic leagues across Europe, the gap between the wealthiest clubs and the rest has widened to the point where the identity of the championship winner is effectively predetermined in most seasons. Grassroots clubs, whose volunteer coaches and modest gate receipts represent the authentic foundation of the sport's global ecosystem, find themselves increasingly marginalised in a conversation dominated by television revenue and billionaire ownership.", "Yet football's capacity for renewal and its deep roots in local identity have repeatedly proven more resilient than its critics anticipate. National teams — whose squads are drawn from communities rather than purchased on a global transfer market — continue to generate moments of collective emotion that club football, for all its glamour, struggles to match. The 2022 World Cup saw Morocco become the first African nation to reach the semi-finals of the tournament, a achievement that reverberated across the continent and among diaspora communities worldwide as something far more significant than a sporting result. Football, at its best, remains a mirror held up to the societies that play it — reflecting their inequalities, their aspirations, and their capacity for transcendent, irrational joy.", "Whether the sport can navigate the tensions between its commercial imperatives and its communal soul is perhaps the defining question of its next chapter. The answer will depend not only on the decisions of administrators, broadcasters, and investors but on the billions of ordinary fans whose passion, ultimately, is the only thing that makes any of it matter. [4]"], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes what the passage is mainly about?", options: ["A broad examination of football's cultural significance, historical spread, economic scale, structural tensions, and enduring communal power.", "A celebration of the FIFA World Cup as the single most important sporting event in human history.", "A comparison of football's popularity in different regions of the world and an explanation of why it is less popular in the United States.", "An argument that the commercialization of modern football has irreparably destroyed the sport's grassroots foundations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, when and where were the first standardized rules of football drawn up?", options: ["In France in 1904, when FIFA was founded as the international governing body of the sport.", "In Uruguay in 1930, when the first FIFA World Cup was organized.", "In England in 1863, when the Football Association separated the sport from rugby.", "In Scotland in 1872, when the first international match between England and Scotland was played."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many people watched the 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar in a single day?", options: ["Approximately 500 million viewers worldwide.", "Around 3.5 billion, representing the sport's total global fanbase.", "An estimated 1.5 billion people.", "More than one billion, making it the most-watched event in television history."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following sporting upsets is NOT mentioned as an example of a weaker team defeating a stronger opponent?", options: ["Cameroon defeating Argentina at the 1990 World Cup.", "Senegal eliminating France in the round of sixteen at the 2002 World Cup.", "Morocco reaching the semi-finals of the 2022 World Cup.", "Iceland holding Portugal in the 2016 European Championship."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what record did Neymar's transfer from Barcelona to Paris Saint-Germain set in 2017?", options: ["It set a record transfer fee of €222 million that has yet to be surpassed.", "It generated a transfer fee of €150 million, the largest ever paid by a French club.", "It was the most expensive transfer involving a South American player at that time.", "It was the first transfer to exceed €100 million, establishing a new benchmark for elite players."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was historically significant about Morocco's performance at the 2022 World Cup?", options: ["Morocco defeated the defending champions in a result widely described as the greatest upset in tournament history.", "Morocco became the first African nation to reach the semi-finals of the World Cup.", "Morocco became the first team from outside Europe or South America to win the World Cup.", "Morocco's goalkeeper set a record for the most saves in a single World Cup tournament."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"definitive\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Controversial and subject to ongoing dispute among governing bodies.", "Most authoritative and conclusive, settling the matter finally.", "Commercially profitable and widely broadcast to international audiences.", "Historically the oldest and most traditional of its kind."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"fantastical\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Carefully calculated to maximize the commercial return for all parties.", "So extraordinary as to seem impossible or beyond imagination.", "Irresponsible and likely to cause long-term financial damage to the sport.", "Admirable and reflecting the extraordinary talent of the players involved."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"reverberated\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Was celebrated briefly before being quickly forgotten.", "Was felt widely and had a lasting resonance far beyond its immediate context.", "Was criticized by opponents who questioned the team's methods.", "Was reported inaccurately by international media outlets."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In England, for instance, Manchester City and Liverpool have won the Premier League title in every season but one since 2018, a dominance that many supporters of other clubs find increasingly difficult to sustain enthusiasm for.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Malaysia: A Nation of Cultural Diversity and Economic Growth", paragraphs: ["Few nations in the world have navigated the transition from colonial dependency to middle-income economic powerhouse with as much speed, and as much unresolved complexity, as Malaysia. Situated at the crossroads of maritime Southeast Asia, Malaysia occupies the southern portion of the Malay Peninsula and the northern part of the island of Borneo, giving it a geographical position that has historically made it both a meeting point of civilizations and a prize for imperial ambitions. [1] Today, with a population of approximately 33 million people drawn from Malay, Chinese, Indian, and indigenous communities — each with its own languages, religious traditions, and cultural practices — Malaysia presents itself to the world as a model of multiracial coexistence, an aspiration whose relationship with reality is, depending on one's vantage point, either an ongoing achievement or an ongoing negotiation.", "The country's ethnic composition is the product of deliberate colonial engineering as much as of organic migration. British colonial administrators, who governed the Malay Peninsula from the late eighteenth century until independence in 1957, brought large numbers of Chinese workers to operate tin mines and Indian laborers to work on rubber plantations, creating an ethnically stratified society whose fault lines have shaped Malaysian politics ever since. The constitutional settlement reached at independence — which granted citizenship to non-Malay communities in exchange for the recognition of special privileges for the indigenous Malay majority, a group known collectively as the Bumiputera — has remained the cornerstone of the country's social contract, though its interpretation and application have been subjects of continuous and sometimes acrimonious debate.", "Economically, Malaysia's trajectory since independence has been one of the more dramatic success stories of the developing world. In 1957, the country was heavily dependent on the export of tin and rubber, commodities whose prices were determined by international markets over which Malaysia had no control. [2] The economic transformation initiated by Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad from 1981 onward shifted the country's focus decisively toward manufacturing and export-led industrialization. By the 1990s, Malaysia had become a major producer of electronics, semiconductors, and automotive components, and Kuala Lumpur's skyline was being reshaped by the Petronas Twin Towers — completed in 1998 and briefly the tallest buildings in the world — which served as a tangible declaration of the country's ambitions to join the ranks of fully developed nations by 2020, a goal enshrined in the national development vision known as Wawasan 2020.", "The 1997 Asian financial crisis administered a severe shock to Malaysia's economic model and to the political authority of Mahathir himself. As the ringgit lost more than a third of its value and foreign capital fled the region, Malaysia defied the prescriptions of the International Monetary Fund by imposing capital controls rather than accepting an austerity bailout — a decision that proved, in the medium term, more effective than the approach taken by several neighboring countries that did accept IMF assistance. The episode revealed both the resilience of Malaysia's economic institutions and the willingness of its leadership to pursue independent policy paths in the face of considerable international pressure. [3]", "Cultural life in Malaysia is as layered as its ethnic composition would suggest. The country's official religion is Islam, which is practised by the Malay majority, but the constitution guarantees freedom of worship to all citizens, and the Chinese and Indian communities maintain vibrant traditions of Buddhist, Taoist, Hindu, and Christian observance. The annual calendar is punctuated by a remarkable succession of public holidays representing the major festivals of each community — Hari Raya Aidilfitri, Chinese New Year, Deepavali, and Christmas are all recognized as national celebrations — a feature of Malaysian public life that visitors frequently cite as one of the country's most distinctive and appealing characteristics. Kuala Lumpur's food culture, in which Malay, Chinese, and Indian culinary traditions have hybridized over generations into a cuisine of extraordinary variety and intensity, is perhaps the most immediate and sensory expression of what Malaysian multiculturalism actually looks and tastes like at street level.", "The limits of this multiculturalism, however, are also real and should not be romanticized. The Bumiputera affirmative action policies, which reserve quotas in university admissions, civil service positions, and business licensing for Malays and other indigenous groups, have been a source of persistent grievance among Chinese and Indian Malaysians who argue that the system systematically disadvantages them regardless of individual merit. Ethnic political parties — whose existence is itself a symptom of a society that has not fully transcended communal identity as the basis of political organization — have historically negotiated the distribution of state resources along ethnic lines in ways that have sometimes deepened rather than bridged social divisions. [4]", "What Malaysia ultimately demonstrates is that diversity, in the absence of strong and trusted institutions, requires constant, active management rather than passive coexistence. The country's ongoing experiment in multiracial governance — imperfect, contested, and frequently frustrating to those who experience its inequities — nevertheless represents one of the more serious and sustained attempts in the postcolonial world to build a functioning plural society from the fragments of a colonial past. Whether the next generation of Malaysians chooses to define itself primarily through its ethnic communities or through a more genuinely shared national identity may be the most consequential political question the country faces in the decades ahead."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Malaysia's cultural diversity is best understood as a legacy of British colonial engineering that has prevented the country from developing a genuine national identity.", "Malaysia illustrates that building a successful multiracial society and a dynamic economy from a colonial legacy is possible but requires ongoing effort and involves persistent tensions that should not be minimized.", "Malaysia's economic miracle proves that former colonial states can achieve developed-nation status within a single generation through strong government leadership.", "The Bumiputera affirmative action policies have been the primary cause of Malaysia's failure to achieve the goals of Wawasan 2020."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, on which two commodities was Malaysia heavily dependent for its exports at the time of independence in 1957?", options: ["Timber and tropical agricultural products.", "Electronics and semiconductor components.", "Palm oil and natural gas.", "Tin and rubber."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following industries is NOT mentioned as a sector that Malaysia developed during its industrialization drive from the 1980s onward?", options: ["Semiconductor production.", "Automotive component manufacturing.", "Pharmaceutical research and development.", "Electronics manufacturing."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did Malaysia's response to the 1997 Asian financial crisis differ from that of some neighboring countries?", options: ["Malaysia imposed capital controls rather than accepting an IMF austerity bailout, an approach that proved effective in the medium term.", "Malaysia sought emergency loans from China rather than from international financial institutions such as the IMF.", "Malaysia avoided the crisis almost entirely because its economy was more diversified than those of neighboring countries.", "Malaysia accepted IMF assistance but negotiated more favorable terms than its neighbors."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following national celebrations is NOT mentioned as a recognized public holiday in Malaysia?", options: ["Vesak Day.", "Hari Raya Aidilfitri.", "Chinese New Year.", "Deepavali."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"cornerstone\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A controversial and widely criticized aspect of the legal system.", "An administrative procedure introduced to manage population growth.", "A temporary compromise reached under pressure that both sides intended to revise.", "The most fundamental and indispensable foundation of something."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"tangible\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Concrete and physically real, able to be seen or experienced directly.", "Financially precarious and dependent on continued foreign investment.", "Controversial and resisted by a significant portion of the population.", "Symbolic only, having no practical function beyond its representational value."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"punctuated\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Marked at intervals by events that break up and give character to a sequence.", "Celebrated with unusual intensity because of the diversity of the population.", "Shortened significantly due to overlapping celebrations across communities.", "Disrupted and made difficult by recurring administrative conflicts."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of Malaysia's multicultural model?", options: ["The author suggests that Malaysia's diversity has been more of a burden than a benefit, and that the country would have developed faster as a more ethnically homogeneous state.", "The author implies that the Bumiputera policies are the fundamental obstacle preventing Malaysia from becoming a genuinely inclusive society.", "The author views Malaysia's multicultural project as a serious and significant achievement that is nonetheless incomplete, contested, and in need of ongoing active management rather than complacent celebration.", "The author believes Malaysia's multicultural model is a complete success that other postcolonial nations should replicate without modification."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This vulnerability to external commodity price shocks was widely recognized by planners as an unsustainable basis for long-term national development, lending urgency to the search for an alternative economic model.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
